elopers have  for  how you should build your neural  networks now  there are a couple other libraries mxnet  which  is supported by amazon and blue cntk the  cognitive  toolkit which was supported by microsoft  and as you can see they're not nearly as  popular though maybe if i did this in  bing  cntk would be a bit more popular than me  doing this in google  and um so other than those you can see  that there's one  other library that is eating into  the uh the share of tensorflow and keras  and that is pie torch in green so that  is officially supported by facebook  and it was initially being developed  just by one gentleman at facebook  and today we have a lot of people using  both  pi torch and tensorflow paris  so in particular looking at job postings  from april of this year  you can see that tensorflow has a  modest lead over pytorch in terms of  being mentioned in the job postings  however across the united states states  in major urban areas  a sizable portion of job postings  uh do mention pytorch relative to the  proportion that mentioned tensorflow so  seattle seems to be particularly a big  lover of  pi torch where 77 of  job postings relative to tensorflow  mention  pytorch and so across these metro areas  there's a 60  average so in either case both of these  libraries are  very prominent definitely the two most  important deep learning libraries  there's one other library that i didn't  have in that google search chart called  cafe  because it turns out people like coffee  a lot more than they like deep learning  libraries so i can't really  uh include that in a google search  so obviously today we're primarily  talking about pi torch and tensorflow so  i'll focus on those two columns here  a big difference between these  two deep learning libraries was that for  up until the tensorflow 2.0 release in  uh late 2019 late last year  tensorflow had what's called a symbolic  programming style which is shared with  cafe and this symbolic programming style 